Likely causes
- Constructor mismatch: `MealDto` has no constructor that accepts `(String,int,int,int,int)`.
- Class not public/abstract/record or wrong package so the `import dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto.MealDto;` doesn't match.
- Compile error earlier (e.g., `TestUtils.asJsonString` uses Gson and the Gson dependency is missing) causing Eclipse to not compile the DTO.
- Missing no-arg constructor needed by your JSON serializer.

Show me the source of `MealDto` and `TestUtils` (or any compile errors from the Problems view). Meanwhile, here’s a simple DTO you can use or adapt; it provides a matching constructor and a no-arg constructor for JSON libraries.

Brief explanation: plain Java DTO with fields, getters/setters, no-arg constructor and the 5-arg constructor used in the test.

```java
// Java
package dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto;

public class MealDto {
    private String name;
    private int calories;
    private int protein;
    private int carbs;
    private int fat;

    public MealDto() {
        // no-arg for serializers
    }

    public MealDto(String name, int calories, int protein, int carbs, int fat) {
        this.name = name;
        this.calories = calories;
        this.protein = protein;
        this.carbs = carbs;
        this.fat = fat;
    }

    public String getName() { return name; }
    public void setName(String name) { this.name = name; }

    public int getCalories() { return calories; }
    public void setCalories(int calories) { this.calories = calories; }

    public int getProtein() { return protein; }
    public void setProtein(int protein) { this.protein = protein; }

    public int getCarbs() { return carbs; }
    public void setCarbs(int carbs) { this.carbs = carbs; }

    public int getFat() { return fat; }
    public void setFat(int fat) { this.fat = fat; }
}
```

