You already have Lombok\`s constructors, but two things can make creating a MealDto with five parameters fail:
- Your test uses a constructor without the \`id\` (signature: \`(String,int,int,int,int)\`) while Lombok\`s \`@AllArgsConstructor\` creates a constructor that includes \`id\` first (\`(Long,String,Integer,Integer,Integer,Integer)\`).
- Or Lombok/annotation processing is not enabled in Eclipse so the generated constructors don't exist at compile time.

Fix: add an explicit 5-arg constructor (no \`id\`) or enable Lombok. Below adds the explicit constructor so code that calls \`new MealDto(name, cals, carbs, protein, fat)\` compiles even if Lombok isn't running.

```java
// java
package dev.jclawlor.FitnessGoals.dto;

import com.fasterxml.jackson.annotation.JsonProperty;

import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotBlank;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.NotEmpty;
import jakarta.validation.constraints.Positive;

import lombok.AllArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Data;
import lombok.Getter;
import lombok.NoArgsConstructor;
import lombok.Setter;

@Getter
@Setter
@NoArgsConstructor
@AllArgsConstructor
public class MealDto {
	private Long id;
	@JsonProperty("name")
	@NotBlank
	private String name;
	@Positive
	private Integer calories;
	@Positive
	private Integer carbohydrates;
	@Positive
	private Integer protein;
	@Positive
	private Integer fat;

	// explicit constructor without id (matches calls like new MealDto(name, cals, carbs, protein, fat))
	public MealDto(String name, Integer calories, Integer carbohydrates, Integer protein, Integer fat) {
		this.name = name;
		this.calories = calories;
		this.carbohydrates = carbohydrates;
		this.protein = protein;
		this.fat = fat;
	}

	public void setId(Long id2) {
		this.id = id2;
	}
}
```
